<ToolPage seo="Should I Water My Lawn Today? Free Rain Meter" crumb="Should I Water?" slug="should-i-water-my-lawn" appName="Should I Water My Lawn? (Rain Meter)"
  h1="Should I Water My Lawn?"
  description="A free rain meter for your ZIP: last week’s rain vs. what your lawn used, plus the forecast, turned into a straight answer. Water, wait, or skip."
  answer="<b>Water when rain has fallen short of what the lawn used by about half an inch or more, and no real rain is coming in the next few days.</b> Then water deep, about half an inch to an inch, early in the morning, once or twice a week. The rain meter below checks your rain and forecast and tells you which."
  faq={faq} sources={sources}>
  <Fragment slot="tool"><RainMeter /></Fragment>

  <h2>How the Rain Meter Works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>It adds up the rain.</strong> Last 7 days for your ZIP, from a free weather service.</li>
    <li><strong>It figures out what your lawn drank.</strong> Grass loses water to sun, heat and wind every day. Weather folks call it evapotranspiration. I call it the lawn sweating.</li>
    <li><strong>It checks the next 3 days.</strong> If real rain is likely, it tells you to wait and let the sky do it for free.</li>
    <li><strong>It gives you one answer.</strong> Not Needed, Wait for Rain, Recommended, or Needed.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Weather models miss pop-up thunderstorms. Got a rain gauge? Type in what it says and the meter uses your number instead.</p>

  <h2>The Watering Cheat Sheet</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>What to Do</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Normal summer week</td><td>About 1 inch total, rain included, in 1 or 2 deep waterings</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Heat wave (90°F+)</td><td>Same inch, early morning. Don’t fertilize. Mow tall or skip a week</td></tr>
      <tr><td>New seed</td><td>Keep the top inch damp: light watering once or twice a day until it’s up</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Spring and fall</td><td>Usually nothing. Rain covers it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Winter</td><td>Nothing. The lawn is asleep</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>How long is an inch? Put a few empty tuna cans on the lawn, run the sprinkler, and time how long it takes to fill them about an inch deep. Most sprinklers take 45 minutes to over an hour.</p>
  <p>More detail in the straight-answer guide: <a href="/guides/how-often-to-water-lawn/">how often to water your lawn</a>.</p>
</ToolPage>
